WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:06.599
I'm preparing for TEF Canada, and I didn't
want to pay for an expensive course.

00:00:06.599 --> 00:00:09.940
So I found OpenExam Hub, at openexamhub.org:

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:13.888
free study plans built from
official, free material.

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:19.445
"Build my free plan" takes me straight to a
sign-up page, so I create my free

00:00:19.445 --> 00:00:21.730
account with my email and a password.

00:00:22.531 --> 00:00:25.539
The account ties my AI plan
and practice to me.

00:00:25.539 --> 00:00:30.787
That stops the same requests being repeated to
burn through the free AI budget, so

00:00:30.787 --> 00:00:35.715
the service stays free for everyone, and my
progress is saved across devices.

00:00:36.516 --> 00:00:37.641
I'm signed in.

00:00:37.641 --> 00:00:43.589
Now I pick TEF Canada, my target level, B2,
and thirty days until my exam.

00:00:44.391 --> 00:00:48.369
I'm not sure of my level yet, so I start
with the placement self-check:

00:00:48.369 --> 00:00:49.658
twelve quick questions.

00:00:50.459 --> 00:00:55.420
Listening with a transcript, reading,
grammar, vocabulary, even writing and

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:56.016
speaking.

00:00:56.016 --> 00:00:57.537
I just answer honestly.

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:03.559
I get an estimated level, a score for each
skill, and what to prioritise, clearly

00:01:03.559 --> 00:01:05.943
labelled as an unofficial self-check.

00:01:06.746 --> 00:01:11.097
Then I build my personalised roadmap,
and it shows how it was made:

00:01:11.097 --> 00:01:15.642
official sources, a copyright check, a
live link check, then the plan.

00:01:16.443 --> 00:01:17.403
Here's my plan.

00:01:17.403 --> 00:01:22.712
Today's tasks come first, with how long each one
takes, and a strip with all thirty

00:01:22.712 --> 00:01:24.375
days, mock exams included.

00:01:25.178 --> 00:01:28.978
When I finish a task, I tick it off, and
it's saved to my account.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:34.905
Below that, I see my progress, my pace, and my
readiness by skill, so I know what

00:01:34.905 --> 00:01:35.981
to focus on next.

00:01:36.782 --> 00:01:41.801
Every resource is a free page from an official
source, with its link check shown,

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:45.271
and the library lets me filter my
exam's shelf by skill.

00:01:46.072 --> 00:01:50.911
Between official sample papers, I can get five
original practice items for any

00:01:50.911 --> 00:01:53.702
skill, and reveal the answers
when I'm ready.

00:01:54.504 --> 00:01:59.644
If I want to remember one, I save it to my
review deck, and it comes back later for

00:01:59.644 --> 00:02:00.759
spaced repetition.

00:02:01.561 --> 00:02:04.517
And the progress coach checks my
pace against the plan.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:14.860
When I come back, I log in on that same
page with my email and password.

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:19.704
My plan and my ticks are right where I left
them, because they're saved to my

00:02:19.704 --> 00:02:20.207
account.

00:02:21.009 --> 00:02:24.748
Free, built on official
sources, and open source.

00:02:24.748 --> 00:02:29.326
That's how I'm preparing for TEF
Canada, at openexamhub.org.
